#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186706216 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b0eefa2-86f9-5434-b86d-bfb92c9a49b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24\\,\\mathbf i-16\\,\\mathbf j-12\\,\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706204} {:answer/id #uuid "2a7bd763-464a-5565-80a5-5620ed3e72e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6\\,\\mathbf i-8\\,\\mathbf j-12\\,\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706208} {:answer/id #uuid "a54b09e7-decb-5251-9b6e-38b9ce776c7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "48\\,\\mathbf i+16\\,\\mathbf j+12\\,\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706227} {:answer/id #uuid "d35df691-d441-58c3-a4bf-663523daec50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12\\,\\mathbf i+8\\,\\mathbf j+6\\,\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706232} {:answer/id #uuid "e8939b0c-1a1d-5c7b-b782-01f064214890" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-12\\,\\mathbf i+8\\,\\mathbf j-6\\,\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706234}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186706232} :answer-field/id #uuid "468e8e4a-1dc0-5255-8e2c-76c78fbe888e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706214}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4f8717f8-1202-5757-8cdd-ca5341ab346b" :question/problem "For the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=x^{3} + y^{2} z^{2} + 2 z\n$$\n\nfind a nonzero vector perpendicular to the level surface through $P=(-2,1,2)$." :question/worked-solution "A nonzero gradient is perpendicular to its level surface. Treat the other variables as constants when taking each partial derivative.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x&=3 x^{2}\\\\f_y&=2 y z^{2}\\\\f_z&=2 y^{2} z + 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate at $P=(-2,1,2)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(P)&=3 \\left(-2\\right)^{2}=12\\\\f_y(P)&=2 \\left(1\\right) \\left(2\\right)^{2}=8\\\\f_z(P)&=2 \\left(1\\right)^{2} \\left(2\\right) + 2=6\\\\\\nabla f(P)&=12\\,\\mathbf i+8\\,\\mathbf j+6\\,\\mathbf k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe gradient is nonzero.\n\nEvery nonzero scalar multiple of this gradient is normal. In particular,\n\n$$\n1\\nabla f(P)=12\\,\\mathbf i+8\\,\\mathbf j+6\\,\\mathbf k.\n$$\n\nSo the listed vector $12\\,\\mathbf i+8\\,\\mathbf j+6\\,\\mathbf k$ is perpendicular to the surface at $P$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186706221 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37d9041f-2b8a-580a-888d-fee59c51e314" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\,\\mathbf i+8\\,\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706209} {:answer/id #uuid "9da4671b-3328-5f8a-a52a-60f9f328d02b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\mathbf j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706226} {:answer/id #uuid "b6101fde-b391-53b1-af92-74090365bfc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8\\,\\mathbf i+4\\,\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706229} {:answer/id #uuid "c951d23e-cb37-5419-b750-77fd3e86ecf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706230} {:answer/id #uuid "cc48eb5b-f20e-5cb2-b6d8-6f4e58073009" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\,\\mathbf i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706231}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186706209} :answer-field/id #uuid "95d7e7a6-7ef3-54de-b1e6-5d891bce879b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706224}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "65ea1e09-a8d8-5e63-8997-991c0e4997d1" :question/problem "For the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=- x^{2} + 2 y^{2} + 2 z^{2}\n$$\n\nfind a nonzero vector perpendicular to the level surface through $P=(2,0,-2)$." :question/worked-solution "A nonzero gradient is perpendicular to its level surface. Treat the other variables as constants when taking each partial derivative.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x&=- 2 x\\\\f_y&=4 y\\\\f_z&=4 z\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate at $P=(2,0,-2)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(P)&=- 2 \\left(2\\right)=-4\\\\f_y(P)&=4 \\left(0\\right)=0\\\\f_z(P)&=4 \\left(-2\\right)=-8\\\\\\nabla f(P)&=-4\\,\\mathbf i-8\\,\\mathbf k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe gradient is nonzero.\n\nEvery nonzero scalar multiple of this gradient is normal. In particular,\n\n$$\n-1\\nabla f(P)=4\\,\\mathbf i+8\\,\\mathbf k.\n$$\n\nSo the listed vector $4\\,\\mathbf i+8\\,\\mathbf k$ is perpendicular to the surface at $P$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186706223 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29fca2c5-d618-54f5-8bcf-32e4b53cf79f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{6}{5}\\,\\mathbf i+\\frac{8}{5}\\,\\mathbf j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706207} {:answer/id #uuid "5d692c91-51ce-5469-bcc9-bdad7dc6018f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\mathbf j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706218} {:answer/id #uuid "9b8654df-e954-52e5-abfd-582743cc9a07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{8}{5}\\,\\mathbf i-\\frac{6}{5}\\,\\mathbf j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706225} {:answer/id #uuid "ebfbf8df-eeef-54c0-87b7-314b7efa55d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\mathbf i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706235} {:answer/id #uuid "f7c2979b-6b90-5f93-be18-c1800db8ae2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706237}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186706207} :answer-field/id #uuid "713c6528-e93a-5733-a772-154df6854821" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706222}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "813437b6-a6b0-5b8e-833c-ffa9abb3b703" :question/problem "For the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2}}\n$$\n\nfind a nonzero vector perpendicular to the level surface through $P=(3,-4,0)$." :question/worked-solution "A nonzero gradient is perpendicular to its level surface. Treat the other variables as constants when taking each partial derivative.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x&=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2}}}\\\\f_y&=\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2}}}\\\\f_z&=\\frac{z}{\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2}}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate at $P=(3,-4,0)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(P)&=\\frac{\\left(3\\right)}{\\sqrt{\\left(3\\right)^{2} + \\left(-4\\right)^{2} + \\left(0\\right)^{2}}}=\\frac{3}{5}\\\\f_y(P)&=\\frac{\\left(-4\\right)}{\\sqrt{\\left(3\\right)^{2} + \\left(-4\\right)^{2} + \\left(0\\right)^{2}}}=- \\frac{4}{5}\\\\f_z(P)&=\\frac{\\left(0\\right)}{\\sqrt{\\left(3\\right)^{2} + \\left(-4\\right)^{2} + \\left(0\\right)^{2}}}=0\\\\\\nabla f(P)&=\\frac{3}{5}\\,\\mathbf i-\\frac{4}{5}\\,\\mathbf j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe gradient is nonzero.\n\nEvery nonzero scalar multiple of this gradient is normal. In particular,\n\n$$\n-2\\nabla f(P)=-\\frac{6}{5}\\,\\mathbf i+\\frac{8}{5}\\,\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nSo the listed vector $-\\frac{6}{5}\\,\\mathbf i+\\frac{8}{5}\\,\\mathbf j$ is perpendicular to the surface at $P$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186706228 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "262299b8-eb82-58a9-ac24-2244552bad7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8\\,\\mathbf i+2\\,\\mathbf j+6\\,\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706206} {:answer/id #uuid "3c2d44e0-5b26-51fe-85fd-93698d5da32c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8\\,\\mathbf i-12\\,\\mathbf j-4\\,\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706210} {:answer/id #uuid "3dde7920-e485-520d-bf76-dd62a5240622" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\,\\mathbf i-2\\,\\mathbf j-6\\,\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706211} {:answer/id #uuid "5b9d6592-6a12-5922-8c7b-7edb4f5aa364" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8\\,\\mathbf i+4\\,\\mathbf j+12\\,\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706217} {:answer/id #uuid "63906530-fc98-50a7-bd28-557c58efdabb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\mathbf i+2\\,\\mathbf j+2\\,\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706220}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186706211} :answer-field/id #uuid "4400a8c6-53b6-561b-94fa-9952c9a6107a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706213}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a597ae32-66ae-530a-915a-dc69b0f2733b" :question/problem "For the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=x^{3} y z - x y z^{2}\n$$\n\nfind a nonzero vector perpendicular to the level surface through $P=(1,2,2)$." :question/worked-solution "A nonzero gradient is perpendicular to its level surface. Treat the other variables as constants when taking each partial derivative.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x&=3 x^{2} y z - y z^{2}\\\\f_y&=x^{3} z - x z^{2}\\\\f_z&=x^{3} y - 2 x y z\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate at $P=(1,2,2)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(P)&=3 \\left(1\\right)^{2} \\left(2\\right) \\left(2\\right) - \\left(2\\right) \\left(2\\right)^{2}=4\\\\f_y(P)&=\\left(1\\right)^{3} \\left(2\\right) - \\left(1\\right) \\left(2\\right)^{2}=-2\\\\f_z(P)&=\\left(1\\right)^{3} \\left(2\\right) - 2 \\left(1\\right) \\left(2\\right) \\left(2\\right)=-6\\\\\\nabla f(P)&=4\\,\\mathbf i-2\\,\\mathbf j-6\\,\\mathbf k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe gradient is nonzero.\n\nEvery nonzero scalar multiple of this gradient is normal. In particular,\n\n$$\n1\\nabla f(P)=4\\,\\mathbf i-2\\,\\mathbf j-6\\,\\mathbf k.\n$$\n\nSo the listed vector $4\\,\\mathbf i-2\\,\\mathbf j-6\\,\\mathbf k$ is perpendicular to the surface at $P$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186706329 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "019c7b98-3749-543a-9e96-68bc97a475aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706319} {:answer/id #uuid "094935f2-8dd9-5b20-95b0-5d1250212479" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4\\,\\mathbf i+4\\,\\mathbf j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706322} {:answer/id #uuid "2f39ab17-f804-5be5-9d2c-5e4aa0d6faed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\mathbf i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706324} {:answer/id #uuid "d7b848b1-3319-52ee-80f8-256bc3d157de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\mathbf j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706342} {:answer/id #uuid "e4700668-58d9-5057-905e-b9bf5c966f85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8\\,\\mathbf i+8\\,\\mathbf j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706344}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186706344} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2f21554-8aa9-5c6b-baa3-71628e64e6d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706338}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "82884b2a-bb55-536c-86e6-b9cbe17dceca" :question/problem "For the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=\\left(2 z^{2} + 2 \\left(x - 1\\right)^{2} + 2 \\left(y + 1\\right)^{2} - 4\\right)^{2}\n$$\n\nfind a nonzero vector perpendicular to the level surface through $P=(2,0,0)$." :question/worked-solution "At the supplied point, $f(P)=0$. Thus the level surface is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left(2 z^{2} + 2 \\left(x - 1\\right)^{2} + 2 \\left(y + 1\\right)^{2} - 4\\right)^{2}&=0\\\\2 z^{2} + 2 \\left(x - 1\\right)^{2} + 2 \\left(y + 1\\right)^{2} - 4&=0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA real number has square zero exactly when it is zero, so these equations describe the same surface. By the chain rule,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla f&=2\\left(2 z^{2} + 2 \\left(x - 1\\right)^{2} + 2 \\left(y + 1\\right)^{2} - 4\\right)^{1}\\nabla g\\\\\\nabla f(P)&=\\mathbf 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe zero vector has no direction. It does not establish that the surface lacks a normal. Use the equivalent defining function\n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=2 z^{2} + 2 \\left(x - 1\\right)^{2} + 2 \\left(y + 1\\right)^{2} - 4\n$$\n\nA nonzero gradient is perpendicular to its level surface. Treat the other variables as constants when taking each partial derivative.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng_x&=4 x - 4\\\\g_y&=4 y + 4\\\\g_z&=4 z\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate at $P=(2,0,0)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng_x(P)&=4 \\left(2\\right) - 4=4\\\\g_y(P)&=4 \\left(0\\right) + 4=4\\\\g_z(P)&=4 \\left(0\\right)=0\\\\\\nabla g(P)&=4\\,\\mathbf i+4\\,\\mathbf j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe gradient is nonzero.\n\nEvery nonzero scalar multiple of this gradient is normal. In particular,\n\n$$\n2\\nabla g(P)=8\\,\\mathbf i+8\\,\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nSo the listed vector $8\\,\\mathbf i+8\\,\\mathbf j$ is perpendicular to the surface at $P$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186706333 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b300463-af88-5957-94d3-7d5903bb846e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24\\,\\mathbf i+24\\,\\mathbf j+24\\,\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706323} {:answer/id #uuid "4dffdc54-6d80-50ae-8cd9-b2091ff3b889" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24\\,\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706326} {:answer/id #uuid "4f73ffa5-cd20-51ec-a0bc-c83e18843f24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706327} {:answer/id #uuid "872ed1c8-ed32-57ce-874b-487de0e3f0ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24\\,\\mathbf i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706331} {:answer/id #uuid "ed0494d5-9dcc-56a7-b0e2-dc44f34fcebc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12\\,\\mathbf j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706345}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186706345} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7ba6c5c-87a0-5966-803a-27e0e125a496" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706339}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "946f8d77-fa6b-5216-a069-aaa616179a6c" :question/problem "For the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=\\left(2 x^{2} + 2 \\left(y - 1\\right)^{2} + \\left(z + 1\\right)^{2} - 1\\right)^{3}\n$$\n\nfind a nonzero vector perpendicular to the level surface through $P=(0,2,-1)$." :question/worked-solution "Write\n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=2 x^{2} + 2 \\left(y - 1\\right)^{2} + \\left(z + 1\\right)^{2} - 1\n$$\n\nso $f=g^{3}$ and $g(P)=1$. Compute the inner gradient, then include the outer power by the chain rule.\n\nA nonzero gradient is perpendicular to its level surface. Treat the other variables as constants when taking each partial derivative.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng_x&=4 x\\\\g_y&=4 y - 4\\\\g_z&=2 z + 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate at $P=(0,2,-1)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng_x(P)&=4 \\left(0\\right)=0\\\\g_y(P)&=4 \\left(2\\right) - 4=4\\\\g_z(P)&=2 \\left(-1\\right) + 2=0\\\\\\nabla g(P)&=4\\,\\mathbf j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe gradient is nonzero.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla f&=3g^{2}\\nabla g\\\\\\nabla f(P)&=3(1)^{2}\\nabla g(P)\\\\&=12\\,\\mathbf j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvery nonzero scalar multiple of this gradient is normal. In particular,\n\n$$\n1\\nabla f(P)=12\\,\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nSo the listed vector $12\\,\\mathbf j$ is perpendicular to the surface at $P$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186706337 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05f855cd-b723-57f0-be94-5cd08d5288e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8\\,\\mathbf i-8\\,\\mathbf j-8\\,\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706320} {:answer/id #uuid "3a61764d-971d-5da3-a0b5-0358ffde281e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\,\\mathbf i-2\\,\\mathbf j-8\\,\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706325} {:answer/id #uuid "8449fa93-1d9e-5e3d-ab4a-fde3794e52e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-16\\,\\mathbf i+8\\,\\mathbf j+8\\,\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706330} {:answer/id #uuid "aa5836da-3c17-5dd0-9abd-6da3dd1b8024" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8\\,\\mathbf i+4\\,\\mathbf j+8\\,\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706334} {:answer/id #uuid "b8dc1d4f-4c9b-586a-bdc7-8e3f50229159" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8\\,\\mathbf i-8\\,\\mathbf j-16\\,\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706336}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186706320} :answer-field/id #uuid "062bc85b-9543-596e-8dcc-f381d142857b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706321}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "ba5ba562-fc26-5f33-8863-5bcc8f5acb7e" :question/problem "For the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=\\left(2 y^{2} + 2 z^{2} + 2 \\left(x - 1\\right)^{2} - 6\\right)^{2}\n$$\n\nfind a nonzero vector perpendicular to the level surface through $P=(2,-1,-1)$." :question/worked-solution "At the supplied point, $f(P)=0$. Thus the level surface is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left(2 y^{2} + 2 z^{2} + 2 \\left(x - 1\\right)^{2} - 6\\right)^{2}&=0\\\\2 y^{2} + 2 z^{2} + 2 \\left(x - 1\\right)^{2} - 6&=0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA real number has square zero exactly when it is zero, so these equations describe the same surface. By the chain rule,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla f&=2\\left(2 y^{2} + 2 z^{2} + 2 \\left(x - 1\\right)^{2} - 6\\right)^{1}\\nabla g\\\\\\nabla f(P)&=\\mathbf 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe zero vector has no direction. It does not establish that the surface lacks a normal. Use the equivalent defining function\n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=2 y^{2} + 2 z^{2} + 2 \\left(x - 1\\right)^{2} - 6\n$$\n\nA nonzero gradient is perpendicular to its level surface. Treat the other variables as constants when taking each partial derivative.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng_x&=4 x - 4\\\\g_y&=4 y\\\\g_z&=4 z\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate at $P=(2,-1,-1)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng_x(P)&=4 \\left(2\\right) - 4=4\\\\g_y(P)&=4 \\left(-1\\right)=-4\\\\g_z(P)&=4 \\left(-1\\right)=-4\\\\\\nabla g(P)&=4\\,\\mathbf i-4\\,\\mathbf j-4\\,\\mathbf k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe gradient is nonzero.\n\nEvery nonzero scalar multiple of this gradient is normal. In particular,\n\n$$\n2\\nabla g(P)=8\\,\\mathbf i-8\\,\\mathbf j-8\\,\\mathbf k.\n$$\n\nSo the listed vector $8\\,\\mathbf i-8\\,\\mathbf j-8\\,\\mathbf k$ is perpendicular to the surface at $P$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186706340 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54913da0-e1e4-5c35-9383-346ec83162c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4\\,\\mathbf i-4\\,\\mathbf j-2\\,\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706328} {:answer/id #uuid "92961717-054a-587f-92e5-dc08f6bc0960" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4\\,\\mathbf i-8\\,\\mathbf j-4\\,\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706332} {:answer/id #uuid "b44d9b55-fcef-5278-a07c-eb662263162f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4\\,\\mathbf i-4\\,\\mathbf j-4\\,\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706335} {:answer/id #uuid "ddd37dd1-1d54-5d89-a9ba-0394cac7a7f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8\\,\\mathbf i+8\\,\\mathbf j+2\\,\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706343} {:answer/id #uuid "faf6d751-d35e-57d0-b904-8122a79dc6aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4\\,\\mathbf i-8\\,\\mathbf j-2\\,\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706346}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186706328} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6661069-efeb-542f-a745-428c6ec4795d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706341}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "cc89904b-f876-5804-b7a5-38db6d2a3507" :question/problem "For the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=\\left(2 \\left(x + 1\\right)^{2} + 2 \\left(y + 1\\right)^{2} + \\left(z - 1\\right)^{2} - 5\\right)^{3}\n$$\n\nfind a nonzero vector perpendicular to the level surface through $P=(-2,-2,0)$." :question/worked-solution "At the supplied point, $f(P)=0$. Thus the level surface is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left(2 \\left(x + 1\\right)^{2} + 2 \\left(y + 1\\right)^{2} + \\left(z - 1\\right)^{2} - 5\\right)^{3}&=0\\\\2 \\left(x + 1\\right)^{2} + 2 \\left(y + 1\\right)^{2} + \\left(z - 1\\right)^{2} - 5&=0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA real number has cube zero exactly when it is zero, so these equations describe the same surface. By the chain rule,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla f&=3\\left(2 \\left(x + 1\\right)^{2} + 2 \\left(y + 1\\right)^{2} + \\left(z - 1\\right)^{2} - 5\\right)^{2}\\nabla g\\\\\\nabla f(P)&=\\mathbf 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe zero vector has no direction. It does not establish that the surface lacks a normal. Use the equivalent defining function\n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=2 \\left(x + 1\\right)^{2} + 2 \\left(y + 1\\right)^{2} + \\left(z - 1\\right)^{2} - 5\n$$\n\nA nonzero gradient is perpendicular to its level surface. Treat the other variables as constants when taking each partial derivative.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng_x&=4 x + 4\\\\g_y&=4 y + 4\\\\g_z&=2 z - 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate at $P=(-2,-2,0)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng_x(P)&=4 \\left(-2\\right) + 4=-4\\\\g_y(P)&=4 \\left(-2\\right) + 4=-4\\\\g_z(P)&=2 \\left(0\\right) - 2=-2\\\\\\nabla g(P)&=-4\\,\\mathbf i-4\\,\\mathbf j-2\\,\\mathbf k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe gradient is nonzero.\n\nEvery nonzero scalar multiple of this gradient is normal. In particular,\n\n$$\n1\\nabla g(P)=-4\\,\\mathbf i-4\\,\\mathbf j-2\\,\\mathbf k.\n$$\n\nSo the listed vector $-4\\,\\mathbf i-4\\,\\mathbf j-2\\,\\mathbf k$ is perpendicular to the surface at $P$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186706348 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14a2dac1-69e6-528d-918e-75c5fb07f3f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\,\\mathbf i-16\\,\\mathbf j-22\\,\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706347} {:answer/id #uuid "bb6ea530-38ca-5c1a-957d-504c0f97fb1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\,\\mathbf i-4\\,\\mathbf j-11\\,\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706368} {:answer/id #uuid "bec698c7-d7c7-5968-9069-68e3b04571ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8\\,\\mathbf i-16\\,\\mathbf j-22\\,\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706369} {:answer/id #uuid "ceca55b5-c407-5908-831a-ef9d1fd2812b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\,\\mathbf i-8\\,\\mathbf j-22\\,\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706370} {:answer/id #uuid "e7114e8a-0f1e-56da-afde-6bb3e9f2c3c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\,\\mathbf i-8\\,\\mathbf j-44\\,\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706374}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186706347} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f04c95c-f30f-53f6-84c2-5a9419b0da1e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706358}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1b7d5425-3927-52b4-bdf5-b4801be4a048" :question/problem "Find a nonzero normal vector to the surface\n\n$$\n- 2 x^{2} + x z + 2 y^{2} + 2 z^{2}=20 - 2 z\n$$\n\nat $P=(1,2,2)$." :question/worked-solution "Move all terms to one side and define\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=- 2 x^{2} + x z + 2 y^{2} + 2 z^{2} + 2 z - 20\n$$\n\nThe point satisfies $F(P)=0$. Include derivatives of the terms moved from the right side.\n\nA nonzero gradient is perpendicular to its level surface. Treat the other variables as constants when taking each partial derivative.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF_x&=- 4 x + z\\\\F_y&=4 y\\\\F_z&=x + 4 z + 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate at $P=(1,2,2)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF_x(P)&=- 4 \\left(1\\right) + \\left(2\\right)=-2\\\\F_y(P)&=4 \\left(2\\right)=8\\\\F_z(P)&=\\left(1\\right) + 4 \\left(2\\right) + 2=11\\\\\\nabla F(P)&=-2\\,\\mathbf i+8\\,\\mathbf j+11\\,\\mathbf k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe gradient is nonzero.\n\nEvery nonzero scalar multiple of this gradient is normal. In particular,\n\n$$\n-2\\nabla F(P)=4\\,\\mathbf i-16\\,\\mathbf j-22\\,\\mathbf k.\n$$\n\nSo the listed vector $4\\,\\mathbf i-16\\,\\mathbf j-22\\,\\mathbf k$ is perpendicular to the surface at $P$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186706353 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1dc50f86-fd54-5688-9566-6ec58d3f051d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\,\\mathbf i+\\mathbf j-4\\,\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706349} {:answer/id #uuid "905ae2bf-4cc3-50b1-96f8-e105ffe45ed4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\,\\mathbf i-\\mathbf j+4\\,\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706360} {:answer/id #uuid "98c02ce8-cf9d-5ddf-9051-37e19082e069" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\,\\mathbf i+\\mathbf j+4\\,\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706361} {:answer/id #uuid "99ecfa88-9dfd-59d0-a140-2c6a278a22ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8\\,\\mathbf i+2\\,\\mathbf j-4\\,\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706362} {:answer/id #uuid "9c05f9e1-53c3-5465-91f1-2a1934e6935b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8\\,\\mathbf i-2\\,\\mathbf j+8\\,\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706364}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186706349} :answer-field/id #uuid "31420ae3-0ae5-5c8e-a5ae-5e97bf12f367" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706351}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "53b91270-5e57-5a59-8870-cc89a50282a2" :question/problem "Find a nonzero normal vector to the surface\n\n$$\nx^{2} - y^{2} + y z - z^{2}=- 2 z - 2\n$$\n\nat $P=(1,0,-1)$." :question/worked-solution "Move all terms to one side and define\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=x^{2} - y^{2} + y z - z^{2} + 2 z + 2\n$$\n\nThe point satisfies $F(P)=0$. Include derivatives of the terms moved from the right side.\n\nA nonzero gradient is perpendicular to its level surface. Treat the other variables as constants when taking each partial derivative.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF_x&=2 x\\\\F_y&=- 2 y + z\\\\F_z&=y - 2 z + 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate at $P=(1,0,-1)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF_x(P)&=2 \\left(1\\right)=2\\\\F_y(P)&=- 2 \\left(0\\right) + \\left(-1\\right)=-1\\\\F_z(P)&=\\left(0\\right) - 2 \\left(-1\\right) + 2=4\\\\\\nabla F(P)&=2\\,\\mathbf i-\\mathbf j+4\\,\\mathbf k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe gradient is nonzero.\n\nEvery nonzero scalar multiple of this gradient is normal. In particular,\n\n$$\n-1\\nabla F(P)=-2\\,\\mathbf i+\\mathbf j-4\\,\\mathbf k.\n$$\n\nSo the listed vector $-2\\,\\mathbf i+\\mathbf j-4\\,\\mathbf k$ is perpendicular to the surface at $P$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186706355 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "645b6e43-1487-5b6c-8fb9-e3022c8e74db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8\\,\\mathbf i-7\\,\\mathbf j-4\\,\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706356} {:answer/id #uuid "9057ccb3-cf10-5f2c-90b7-b33af22dbc6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8\\,\\mathbf i+7\\,\\mathbf j+8\\,\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706359} {:answer/id #uuid "a6f7c2f7-9b45-53ec-94bf-7defddb3f14b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4\\,\\mathbf i+7\\,\\mathbf j+4\\,\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706365} {:answer/id #uuid "a811899e-860f-5f23-898a-4d149d6e5ac1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16\\,\\mathbf i-7\\,\\mathbf j-8\\,\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706366} {:answer/id #uuid "ada8a0cc-8cac-50cd-a0c4-565ebe6e9b54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8\\,\\mathbf i+14\\,\\mathbf j+4\\,\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706367}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186706367} :answer-field/id #uuid "5656220a-5ec3-565f-a7ef-0fd66e42d943" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706354}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5a43e310-669f-58d3-8525-d2049cd21335" :question/problem "Find a nonzero normal vector to the surface\n\n$$\nx^{2} + 2 y^{2} - y z + z^{2}=2 z + 13\n$$\n\nat $P=(2,-2,-1)$." :question/worked-solution "Move all terms to one side and define\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=x^{2} + 2 y^{2} - y z + z^{2} - 2 z - 13\n$$\n\nThe point satisfies $F(P)=0$. Include derivatives of the terms moved from the right side.\n\nA nonzero gradient is perpendicular to its level surface. Treat the other variables as constants when taking each partial derivative.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF_x&=2 x\\\\F_y&=4 y - z\\\\F_z&=- y + 2 z - 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate at $P=(2,-2,-1)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF_x(P)&=2 \\left(2\\right)=4\\\\F_y(P)&=4 \\left(-2\\right) - \\left(-1\\right)=-7\\\\F_z(P)&=- \\left(-2\\right) + 2 \\left(-1\\right) - 2=-2\\\\\\nabla F(P)&=4\\,\\mathbf i-7\\,\\mathbf j-2\\,\\mathbf k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe gradient is nonzero.\n\nEvery nonzero scalar multiple of this gradient is normal. In particular,\n\n$$\n-2\\nabla F(P)=-8\\,\\mathbf i+14\\,\\mathbf j+4\\,\\mathbf k.\n$$\n\nSo the listed vector $-8\\,\\mathbf i+14\\,\\mathbf j+4\\,\\mathbf k$ is perpendicular to the surface at $P$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186706357 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "440ca821-a203-5981-b90c-7dec06500948" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6\\,\\mathbf i-4\\,\\mathbf j-7\\,\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706352} {:answer/id #uuid "9b16c42c-33fc-55e4-8f00-a7813834b039" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\,\\mathbf i-6\\,\\mathbf j-7\\,\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706363} {:answer/id #uuid "d262ada8-3696-5c4f-af65-e4ce10137f83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6\\,\\mathbf i+4\\,\\mathbf j-7\\,\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706371} {:answer/id #uuid "e00a481a-474a-5e1b-87bc-c0f0cf2dab7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12\\,\\mathbf i-4\\,\\mathbf j+7\\,\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706372} {:answer/id #uuid "e0fea486-518e-54a4-8b26-a1773869284a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\,\\mathbf i+4\\,\\mathbf j-7\\,\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706373}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186706371} :answer-field/id #uuid "24049ac0-ce1c-5720-bb9b-78a3e2a01786" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706350}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8ec29a45-e9f4-501b-9608-2033a3c8bfa4" :question/problem "Find a nonzero normal vector to the surface\n\n$$\n- 2 x^{2} + x z + y^{2} - 2 z^{2}=- z - 10\n$$\n\nat $P=(-2,-2,-2)$." :question/worked-solution "Move all terms to one side and define\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=- 2 x^{2} + x z + y^{2} - 2 z^{2} + z + 10\n$$\n\nThe point satisfies $F(P)=0$. Include derivatives of the terms moved from the right side.\n\nA nonzero gradient is perpendicular to its level surface. Treat the other variables as constants when taking each partial derivative.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF_x&=- 4 x + z\\\\F_y&=2 y\\\\F_z&=x - 4 z + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate at $P=(-2,-2,-2)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF_x(P)&=- 4 \\left(-2\\right) + \\left(-2\\right)=6\\\\F_y(P)&=2 \\left(-2\\right)=-4\\\\F_z(P)&=\\left(-2\\right) - 4 \\left(-2\\right) + 1=7\\\\\\nabla F(P)&=6\\,\\mathbf i-4\\,\\mathbf j+7\\,\\mathbf k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe gradient is nonzero.\n\nEvery nonzero scalar multiple of this gradient is normal. In particular,\n\n$$\n-1\\nabla F(P)=-6\\,\\mathbf i+4\\,\\mathbf j-7\\,\\mathbf k.\n$$\n\nSo the listed vector $-6\\,\\mathbf i+4\\,\\mathbf j-7\\,\\mathbf k$ is perpendicular to the surface at $P$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186706376 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17f23830-dfd6-5779-8028-e69fc97e1b68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-24\\,\\mathbf i+12\\,\\mathbf j-24\\,\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706379} {:answer/id #uuid "2f8a673b-5542-5606-b8c2-1e1d9e00d80c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24\\,\\mathbf i-24\\,\\mathbf j-24\\,\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706382} {:answer/id #uuid "6b10bdf3-b2c0-58f0-b1b2-85439aa68762" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-12\\,\\mathbf i+24\\,\\mathbf j+24\\,\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706388} {:answer/id #uuid "79a8bc65-ce0a-5b5d-83af-e14ae43312ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\,\\mathbf i+12\\,\\mathbf j-12\\,\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706389} {:answer/id #uuid "992c7f42-a38a-5db0-8a1f-1ef461dcbdfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\,\\mathbf i+12\\,\\mathbf j+12\\,\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706393}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186706388} :answer-field/id #uuid "3a8707a3-65cf-5431-a1b7-4adb66284577" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706383}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "05636088-390b-56a3-aa46-f2f36d7f0fa7" :question/problem "For the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=\\left(\\left(x - 1\\right)^{2} + 2 \\left(y + 1\\right)^{2} + 2 \\left(z - 1\\right)^{2} - 4\\right)^{3}\n$$\n\nfind a nonzero vector perpendicular to the level surface through $P=(2,-2,0)$." :question/worked-solution "Write\n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=\\left(x - 1\\right)^{2} + 2 \\left(y + 1\\right)^{2} + 2 \\left(z - 1\\right)^{2} - 4\n$$\n\nso $f=g^{3}$ and $g(P)=1$. Compute the inner gradient, then include the outer power by the chain rule.\n\nA nonzero gradient is perpendicular to its level surface. Treat the other variables as constants when taking each partial derivative.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng_x&=2 x - 2\\\\g_y&=4 y + 4\\\\g_z&=4 z - 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate at $P=(2,-2,0)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng_x(P)&=2 \\left(2\\right) - 2=2\\\\g_y(P)&=4 \\left(-2\\right) + 4=-4\\\\g_z(P)&=4 \\left(0\\right) - 4=-4\\\\\\nabla g(P)&=2\\,\\mathbf i-4\\,\\mathbf j-4\\,\\mathbf k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe gradient is nonzero.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla f&=3g^{2}\\nabla g\\\\\\nabla f(P)&=3(1)^{2}\\nabla g(P)\\\\&=6\\,\\mathbf i-12\\,\\mathbf j-12\\,\\mathbf k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvery nonzero scalar multiple of this gradient is normal. In particular,\n\n$$\n-2\\nabla f(P)=-12\\,\\mathbf i+24\\,\\mathbf j+24\\,\\mathbf k.\n$$\n\nSo the listed vector $-12\\,\\mathbf i+24\\,\\mathbf j+24\\,\\mathbf k$ is perpendicular to the surface at $P$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186706378 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0096f1ee-a76f-533f-8063-0a2b44fb34c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-16\\,\\mathbf i-16\\,\\mathbf j-16\\,\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706375} {:answer/id #uuid "20b2dbb4-7fba-532a-a404-f7c46b5d4e82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706380} {:answer/id #uuid "7f9c59d2-e40a-591b-9697-ade49f1209bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16\\,\\mathbf i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706390} {:answer/id #uuid "b78c9773-5aee-529e-8c3a-6a0b5e3a82f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-16\\,\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706395} {:answer/id #uuid "be12aee2-cd20-594d-9145-091fb03ceaee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-16\\,\\mathbf j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706396}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186706390} :answer-field/id #uuid "979cea4c-7f0f-5816-a078-877290eea1d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706392}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "1595db6c-283f-5f7b-a2fc-c9046614cc8c" :question/problem "For the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=\\left(2 \\left(x - 1\\right)^{2} + 2 \\left(y - 1\\right)^{2} + \\left(z + 1\\right)^{2} - 3\\right)^{2}\n$$\n\nfind a nonzero vector perpendicular to the level surface through $P=(2,1,-1)$." :question/worked-solution "Write\n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=2 \\left(x - 1\\right)^{2} + 2 \\left(y - 1\\right)^{2} + \\left(z + 1\\right)^{2} - 3\n$$\n\nso $f=g^{2}$ and $g(P)=-1$. Compute the inner gradient, then include the outer power by the chain rule.\n\nA nonzero gradient is perpendicular to its level surface. Treat the other variables as constants when taking each partial derivative.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng_x&=4 x - 4\\\\g_y&=4 y - 4\\\\g_z&=2 z + 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate at $P=(2,1,-1)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng_x(P)&=4 \\left(2\\right) - 4=4\\\\g_y(P)&=4 \\left(1\\right) - 4=0\\\\g_z(P)&=2 \\left(-1\\right) + 2=0\\\\\\nabla g(P)&=4\\,\\mathbf i\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe gradient is nonzero.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla f&=2g^{1}\\nabla g\\\\\\nabla f(P)&=2(-1)^{1}\\nabla g(P)\\\\&=-8\\,\\mathbf i\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvery nonzero scalar multiple of this gradient is normal. In particular,\n\n$$\n-2\\nabla f(P)=16\\,\\mathbf i.\n$$\n\nSo the listed vector $16\\,\\mathbf i$ is perpendicular to the surface at $P$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186706387 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c16b638-0e47-5262-a469-cc7be65da476" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8\\,\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706381} {:answer/id #uuid "45aea1ec-0ce2-51ba-8153-2c51f76711ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\,\\mathbf j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706385} {:answer/id #uuid "84b9a6f5-da4b-5dea-8623-1537eae27eac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706391} {:answer/id #uuid "a14438dc-d9bb-543e-b27f-097b66d64fba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8\\,\\mathbf i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706394} {:answer/id #uuid "d09200c9-d899-5b34-a619-ec486cf6a301" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8\\,\\mathbf i+8\\,\\mathbf j+8\\,\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706397}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186706381} :answer-field/id #uuid "4db3cde9-5d0f-5c83-9a84-ee60ac095222" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706386}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "623e2dde-6e6a-53f0-9943-eab4677f49f0" :question/problem "For the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=\\left(2 \\left(x - 1\\right)^{2} + 2 \\left(y - 1\\right)^{2} + \\left(z - 1\\right)^{2}\\right)^{2}\n$$\n\nfind a nonzero vector perpendicular to the level surface through $P=(1,1,0)$." :question/worked-solution "Write\n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=2 \\left(x - 1\\right)^{2} + 2 \\left(y - 1\\right)^{2} + \\left(z - 1\\right)^{2}\n$$\n\nso $f=g^{2}$ and $g(P)=1$. Compute the inner gradient, then include the outer power by the chain rule.\n\nA nonzero gradient is perpendicular to its level surface. Treat the other variables as constants when taking each partial derivative.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng_x&=4 x - 4\\\\g_y&=4 y - 4\\\\g_z&=2 z - 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate at $P=(1,1,0)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng_x(P)&=4 \\left(1\\right) - 4=0\\\\g_y(P)&=4 \\left(1\\right) - 4=0\\\\g_z(P)&=2 \\left(0\\right) - 2=-2\\\\\\nabla g(P)&=-2\\,\\mathbf k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe gradient is nonzero.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla f&=2g^{1}\\nabla g\\\\\\nabla f(P)&=2(1)^{1}\\nabla g(P)\\\\&=-4\\,\\mathbf k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvery nonzero scalar multiple of this gradient is normal. In particular,\n\n$$\n2\\nabla f(P)=-8\\,\\mathbf k.\n$$\n\nSo the listed vector $-8\\,\\mathbf k$ is perpendicular to the surface at $P$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186706398 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06dfd4c8-f7b9-567c-8fd0-6c3240e1fd51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\mathbf i+\\mathbf j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706377} {:answer/id #uuid "3f41d9cb-6ae8-50a3-a563-87e1b36f1d2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4\\,\\mathbf i+2\\,\\mathbf j+4\\,\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706384} {:answer/id #uuid "e547006e-541b-5134-98e8-c55d07ed70fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\,\\mathbf i-4\\,\\mathbf j-2\\,\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706399} {:answer/id #uuid "ea219187-7383-5d51-9b96-e84aa2719728" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\,\\mathbf i-2\\,\\mathbf j-4\\,\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706400} {:answer/id #uuid "ee9b0e3c-7186-50e8-bac3-ac97cc9fb3ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\,\\mathbf i-2\\,\\mathbf j-4\\,\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706401}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186706400} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc8e9269-7736-5184-b8c7-7dc9ed718bb2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186706402}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "d385ba5f-d5ba-547f-969a-7071f7ecf211" :question/problem "For the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=\\left(x^{2} + y^{2} + 2 \\left(z - 1\\right)^{2} - 4\\right)^{2}\n$$\n\nfind a nonzero vector perpendicular to the level surface through $P=(1,-1,0)$." :question/worked-solution "At the supplied point, $f(P)=0$. Thus the level surface is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left(x^{2} + y^{2} + 2 \\left(z - 1\\right)^{2} - 4\\right)^{2}&=0\\\\x^{2} + y^{2} + 2 \\left(z - 1\\right)^{2} - 4&=0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA real number has square zero exactly when it is zero, so these equations describe the same surface. By the chain rule,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla f&=2\\left(x^{2} + y^{2} + 2 \\left(z - 1\\right)^{2} - 4\\right)^{1}\\nabla g\\\\\\nabla f(P)&=\\mathbf 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe zero vector has no direction. It does not establish that the surface lacks a normal. Use the equivalent defining function\n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=x^{2} + y^{2} + 2 \\left(z - 1\\right)^{2} - 4\n$$\n\nA nonzero gradient is perpendicular to its level surface. Treat the other variables as constants when taking each partial derivative.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng_x&=2 x\\\\g_y&=2 y\\\\g_z&=4 z - 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate at $P=(1,-1,0)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng_x(P)&=2 \\left(1\\right)=2\\\\g_y(P)&=2 \\left(-1\\right)=-2\\\\g_z(P)&=4 \\left(0\\right) - 4=-4\\\\\\nabla g(P)&=2\\,\\mathbf i-2\\,\\mathbf j-4\\,\\mathbf k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe gradient is nonzero.\n\nEvery nonzero scalar multiple of this gradient is normal. In particular,\n\n$$\n1\\nabla g(P)=2\\,\\mathbf i-2\\,\\mathbf j-4\\,\\mathbf k.\n$$\n\nSo the listed vector $2\\,\\mathbf i-2\\,\\mathbf j-4\\,\\mathbf k$ is perpendicular to the surface at $P$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707980 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "94292091-b506-5b6f-b74a-c9006a4e2208" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/04/04567f14efa5531395cf277be71cabc7cd7322c98c19cd89bfbcf3a757181062.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707989} {:answer/id #uuid "a1c7d01e-0952-5dc0-8aa6-fe7d2b2eeab3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/67/67f650849f5377c19bdf71a6967bd89f7335ba7e551d163ad584cf46b2240f3c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707990} {:answer/id #uuid "ca7c076b-2479-550d-be0b-c4bf48bbaab3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/d3/d36683314c902bdc2a478e053349dae10ba91deecc020eb1c260dade3915ce75.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707993} {:answer/id #uuid "cf5914c8-a1b1-51c5-8057-7a69b8ac8446" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/b4/b40cddf0bed3ad6aefded3f4c56449f19a25257f7b3230961840ef162a4c3afe.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707994} {:answer/id #uuid "dec26261-6c81-58bb-9a15-dbd047c23b10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/00/0041df57116921c426a959d6480ca3cd5af4e9de801ac5800e81c5863b814e8d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707995}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707993} :answer-field/id #uuid "439ef0ad-806f-5d40-a3d1-6c2a8144a080" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707981}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2b0745ca-9ce1-563d-a607-330f73b40973" :question/problem "The diagram shows the gradient vector field of a function $f(x,y)$. Which of the following could be a contour plot of $f$? Arrows show directions only.\n\n![Given nonradial outward gradient vector field](images/a5/a5ab7ab1e55a4c73fe75be5b4c82cab6ebec9404698487d510af7b5666c8160b.png)" :question/worked-solution "Level curves must cross the gradient vectors at right angles. Away from the axes, the arrows point more steeply upward or downward than radial arrows: near $(1,1)$ their direction is proportional to $(1,4)$, not $(1,1)$. Thus circles do not fit.\n\nThe matching curves are ellipses stretched horizontally. On their upper-right arcs the tangents slope gently downward, perpendicular to the steep upward-right arrows. Vertically stretched ellipses have the wrong tangent directions. The hyperbolas and parabolas also fail the perpendicularity check across the diagram.\n\n![Horizontally stretched ellipses and their normal vectors](images/95/955b541e62f4e6ecf05f19e58c36763d6f946961eafe877497a88de4fe450e5b.png)\n\nOne compatible function is $f(x,y)=x^2+4y^2$. Its contours $x^2+4y^2=c$ are horizontally stretched ellipses, and its gradient $(2x,8y)$ has the displayed directions. Values increase outward. Finding this formula is a check, not a required response."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707986 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "153c265e-f363-5b54-b639-8a1afdb39887" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/9b/9bfe92c07e9733131f05abdfa00df4079349d834ec1c4068564de5894b3d89f1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707977} {:answer/id #uuid "7053baf0-3407-5c90-bd97-c1d205730c55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/96/96306cd61dd9cf0ca98d3e358cbda0daf754801b8bdde4f063b7ef6709b1a589.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707985} {:answer/id #uuid "b6dbb20f-3cb8-5d0f-8035-76959fcc4869" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/83/83b4708bb180dda39b77e8ce41aabfffe390f805d0c6cc779eedd16a643cee09.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707991} {:answer/id #uuid "b8f32ea9-a7b8-52ac-913f-5893a22a5a80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/f3/f31bda27ee406677ce2b95e4f9ddb28be5488f20e64355008f394a0f3678b4fc.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707992} {:answer/id #uuid "f147df69-6871-5306-8df4-4277ac3a6696" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/6f/6f2514f12f711dd46d3b8061d2da0572523f2137543b15a4991589c7bd60558d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707996}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707992} :answer-field/id #uuid "68bd1e59-4a9a-5795-bca8-abaa307a58fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707984}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "71befcb7-d04e-5887-9990-c1e281fac3fe" :question/problem "Which of the following could be the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)=y^2-x$? Use the relationship between level curves and gradient directions. Arrows show directions only." :question/worked-solution "First identify the level curves. Setting $f(x,y)=c$ gives\n\n$$\ny^2-x=c,\\qquad x=y^2-c.\n$$\n\nThese are right-opening parabolas. For example, $c=-1,0,1$ gives $x=y^2+1$, $x=y^2$, and $x=y^2-1$.\n\n![Labeled right-opening parabolic contours](images/a8/a886765cec9e09347d56dbfc948167ae35fd2fcae5a1a2907f4f7ed8464019de.png)\n\nFor fixed $y$, decreasing $x$ increases $f$, so the gradient must have a leftward component. Above the $x$-axis, increasing $y$ increases $y^2$, so the vectors point upward-left. Below the axis, decreasing $y$ increases $y^2$, so they point downward-left. On the axis they point directly left. These directions must also be perpendicular to the parabolas.\n\n![Gradient vectors normal to the parabolic contours and pointing toward larger labels](images/3e/3e635f50ea59c6f05af0b9f37f8f32fa15b7436ec93486b4a4cb7b7b6a2155dc.png)\n\nTherefore choose the field with leftward arrows on the $x$-axis, upward-left arrows above it, and downward-left arrows below it. A derivative check gives $\\nabla f=(-1,2y)$. The reversed field points toward decreasing values; the field that points down above the axis has the wrong vertical sign; the rotated parabolic field has the wrong contour orientation; the field tangent to the parabolas fails perpendicularity."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707988 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e3e3ccb-c1a3-5437-b6c6-af187003d465" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/26/2622985ed6af485be1c50403a51bd06d86e098a9a708ade07019618b99d73820.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707979} {:answer/id #uuid "45a6bcd7-9379-5915-9e2b-7b5a9ff389c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/4a/4a3c2b6cf6bc9595f3ec374236adfb342ae1bc907cdb6779079a48f5e87da218.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707982} {:answer/id #uuid "5163f699-56be-5279-91c8-23903e8c3604" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/cd/cdd9ff9a9ad218921b0df8bc5a00941ff587e19c2562898e7ce32c9783cc3ef3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707983} {:answer/id #uuid "7b3aeb6d-e790-5adc-b1e2-f80095b41f08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/4a/4a772e0990f73753defa8ed6398d0d4e40fac11e403b119672fab424cf2e6a12.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707987} {:answer/id #uuid "f41e6196-4b7f-5031-a153-53a7a28b1e1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/d4/d4a04daa5f60576cd5517f71d8383e82f40fdeff010b7760d1d0d3afe3a83189.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707997}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707983} :answer-field/id #uuid "1659a41b-b08c-5b17-bd0c-d52d986758c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186707978}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7c821600-c030-591d-8a36-91a4dbdb76b1" :question/problem "The labeled lines are level curves of a function $f(x,y)$.\n\n![Labeled vertical level curves](images/c0/c0a3f9e3b2b3908d90ae86451d4054fe7f883fb29092753907d2111a79399cbb.png)\n\nRecall that gradient vectors are perpendicular to level curves and point toward increasing values of $f$. Which diagram could show the gradient vector field? Arrows show directions only." :question/worked-solution "Each level curve is vertical, so its gradient vectors must be horizontal. The labels increase from right to left: $-2,-1,0,1,2$. Therefore the gradient vectors point left, toward larger values of $f$.\n\n![Leftward vectors perpendicular to the labeled contours](images/f3/f3391d8805a64f907f1b854887370e94530e7cd278b7d730db9799a8ddb12217.png)\n\nThe correct diagram has leftward arrows everywhere. Rightward arrows point toward decreasing values; upward and downward arrows are tangent to the contours; diagonal arrows are not perpendicular."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708204 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cbad3c1d-3336-5dea-8308-46648e58dae2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(1+xy)e^{xy-z}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186708216}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708216} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c8afc95-3384-5999-89d0-14340dae2417" :answer-field/key "component-x" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186708209} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d1ea495a-4b48-50f0-9b22-8ab597d51e2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x^2e^{xy-z}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186708217}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708217} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad7384ac-9292-5f82-b954-f3c231424a9f" :answer-field/key "component-y" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186708211} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "168440d9-ebaf-5e59-9176-65b5ced1b724" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-xe^{xy-z}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186708199}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708199} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2cdc32d-16e5-53b2-98f4-f33dc561bb67" :answer-field/key "component-z" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186708218}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "50ab6734-01e8-5297-a184-bcbd0ee2dea7" :question/problem "Find the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y,z)=x e^{xy-z}$.\n\nEnter its three component functions:\n\n$\\nabla f(x,y,z)=$ {{component-x}} $\\,i+$ {{component-y}} $\\,j+$ {{component-z}} $\\,k$." :question/worked-solution "Compute the partial derivatives, holding the other variables constant. In the $x$ derivative, both the factor $x$ and the exponent depend on $x$, so use the product rule as well as the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y,z)&=1\\cdot e^{xy-z}+x e^{xy-z}\\cdot y\\\\\n&=(1+xy)e^{xy-z},\\\\\nf_y(x,y,z)&=x e^{xy-z}\\cdot x=x^2e^{xy-z},\\\\\nf_z(x,y,z)&=x e^{xy-z}\\cdot(-1)=-x e^{xy-z}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the gradient vector field is\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y,z)=(1+xy)e^{xy-z}\\,i+x^2e^{xy-z}\\,j-xe^{xy-z}\\,k.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708213 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d713188-6905-5173-b3c4-5cad5671b984" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\cos(xy-2z)\\left(y\\,i+x\\,j-2z\\,k\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186708202} {:answer/id #uuid "7f42543a-a6c5-5648-acb1-81ca8f5dddfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\cos(xy-2z)\\left(y\\,i+x\\,j-2\\,k\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186708206} {:answer/id #uuid "88d6977e-fce7-54a8-b451-3911de99b1ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\cos(xy-2z)\\left(i+j+k\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186708207} {:answer/id #uuid "8fd970c3-12d8-56ac-b00f-25ea747ac843" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\cos(xy-2z)\\left(x\\,i+y\\,j-2\\,k\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186708208} {:answer/id #uuid "b5e4a4bc-f443-5f14-8f51-f23f8b797192" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sin(xy-2z)\\left(y\\,i+x\\,j-2\\,k\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186708212}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708206} :answer-field/id #uuid "70f889ce-79f4-534e-8f71-f29b0b189824" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186708205}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b5f6ba14-d0cf-5802-b427-5e13a730d398" :question/problem "Find the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y,z)=\\sin(xy-2z)$." :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field is $\\nabla f=f_x\\,i+f_y\\,j+f_z\\,k$.\n\nFor each partial derivative, differentiate the outer sine and multiply by the partial derivative of its argument. The other variables are held constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y,z)&=\\cos(xy-2z)\\cdot y=y\\cos(xy-2z),\\\\\nf_y(x,y,z)&=\\cos(xy-2z)\\cdot x=x\\cos(xy-2z),\\\\\nf_z(x,y,z)&=\\cos(xy-2z)\\cdot(-2)=-2\\cos(xy-2z).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y,z)=\\cos(xy-2z)\\left(y\\,i+x\\,j-2\\,k\\right).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708214 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "067e5c6e-5e90-568d-97ec-d5911e0dac0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3x-2y}\\,i+\\frac{1}{3x-2y}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186708198} {:answer/id #uuid "3823875c-c508-5905-8d39-aa97323bacd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3x-2y}\\,i+\\frac{3}{3x-2y}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186708201} {:answer/id #uuid "4a240551-1229-519a-a358-38abe9925183" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{3x-2y}\\,i+\\frac{2}{3x-2y}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186708203} {:answer/id #uuid "a3b72346-50e4-5846-97b1-3f59ecb087a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{3x-2y}\\,i-\\frac{2}{3x-2y}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186708210} {:answer/id #uuid "c48c12df-a486-592c-9d47-4d16e10596f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{3x}\\,i-\\frac{2}{2y}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186708215}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708210} :answer-field/id #uuid "24505429-5067-5850-8b08-acd5d3a9ae61" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186708200}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b88b8854-5147-52bb-b5a2-dac836c45535" :question/problem "Find the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)=\\ln(3x-2y)$ on $3x-2y>0$." :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field is $\\nabla f=f_x\\,i+f_y\\,j$. When taking each partial derivative, hold the other variable constant.\n\nBy the chain rule,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=\\frac{1}{3x-2y}\\cdot 3=\\frac{3}{3x-2y},\\\\\nf_y(x,y)&=\\frac{1}{3x-2y}\\cdot(-2)=-\\frac{2}{3x-2y}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y)=\\frac{3}{3x-2y}\\,i-\\frac{2}{3x-2y}\\,j.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708222 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01c5f349-9576-54c2-b336-cc53a0fea732" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/78/78e298990e4de0dbc58883097dd88fcbe5338b8169ca34d166c7a395f73eb177.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186708219} {:answer/id #uuid "3a429f6a-d737-52d1-a572-12e0f8aa36bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/52/522f72787a12f92cde753ff2bdd86e4bd53e62c995ad29c861b96e3c0ddb22ab.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186708227} {:answer/id #uuid "66b230f0-70af-5f88-9e67-8a83303a5b25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/39/398102b2c33d99162a9b99f74b9c8db5b8b4cd84715353c457e58a742c4c17b1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186708232} {:answer/id #uuid "7947ba95-5d40-5d43-93a0-c094b3cec93d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/3d/3db952065d5ecfade2b25e8a240b05bd876758f0213dfae7f1e436e230eb0d5b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186708237} {:answer/id #uuid "e0e9636e-c976-58df-a289-af2f441408b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/96/96c710e06e5099240aa2c79b3fb3c857a05e6404e38b204d5cfa20a075c2518c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186708249}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708237} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c232e59-98dd-569e-ab24-d96791429be9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186708231}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "19dc6a33-b2ed-58b5-9714-74e56aa52535" :question/problem "The diagram shows the gradient vector field of a function $f(x,y)$. Which of the following could be a contour plot of $f$? Arrows show directions only.\n\n![Given gradient directions](images/e5/e5b0e58975a56cfa2aa4523da04e3d7f0ad9b52e5cedf4e894e264351cefbd86.png)" :question/worked-solution "The field must be perpendicular to every regular level curve. One compatible function, used here to check the geometric match, is $f(x,y)=4\\left(x+1\\right)^2+y^2$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y)&=c\\\\4\\left(x+1\\right)^2+y^2&=c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThese level curves are ellipses stretched vertically. Values increase away from the center $(-1,0)$. Compare oblique points too: the two coordinate directions are weighted in the ratio $4:1$. Unlabeled contours do not specify which side has larger values; the required test for these choices is perpendicularity, including at off-axis points.\n\nA derivative check gives $\\nabla f=\\left\\langle 8\\left(x+1\\right),2y\\right\\rangle$. These directions are normal to the level curves and point toward increasing values. Other curve shapes have a different normal direction somewhere; a reversed field, if offered, points downhill.\n\n![Matching contours and gradient directions](images/bf/bf2ea7634824a019093c9f09fd33da405efd49ed0154fe78003c9e0867ef13e8.png)\n\nTherefore choose this diagram:\n\n![Correct diagram](images/3d/3db952065d5ecfade2b25e8a240b05bd876758f0213dfae7f1e436e230eb0d5b.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708224 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2efd137c-d3ce-5bd7-aa3b-a72122af8299" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/78/78e298990e4de0dbc58883097dd88fcbe5338b8169ca34d166c7a395f73eb177.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186708225} {:answer/id #uuid "b8217785-915c-5f2a-a9b0-90a8f58f9385" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/52/522f72787a12f92cde753ff2bdd86e4bd53e62c995ad29c861b96e3c0ddb22ab.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186708246} {:answer/id #uuid "dbac1e3a-a503-5aae-8bf3-7c5d6ad0e816" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/96/96c710e06e5099240aa2c79b3fb3c857a05e6404e38b204d5cfa20a075c2518c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186708248} {:answer/id #uuid "e613af10-91e6-56c6-8b2e-e45fc0af907d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/39/398102b2c33d99162a9b99f74b9c8db5b8b4cd84715353c457e58a742c4c17b1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186708250} {:answer/id #uuid "e8a3a4cf-0e17-53a5-be8d-dc53976ccf7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/3d/3db952065d5ecfade2b25e8a240b05bd876758f0213dfae7f1e436e230eb0d5b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186708251}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708250} :answer-field/id #uuid "66c3787e-65b6-550c-972e-2df9f36a765c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186708234}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "25067748-5938-5e8a-826c-19b80ffa8371" :question/problem "The diagram shows the gradient vector field of a function $f(x,y)$. Which of the following could be a contour plot of $f$? Arrows show directions only.\n\n![Given gradient directions](images/85/85f3b3ff78aeafd15ceed7338d0a9ae5b532a09e29b248ffda9048aa102e5ab6.png)" :question/worked-solution "The field must be perpendicular to every regular level curve. One compatible function, used here to check the geometric match, is $f(x,y)=y^2-\\left(x+1\\right)$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y)&=c\\\\y^2-\\left(x+1\\right)&=c\\\\x&=-1-c+y^2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThese level curves are right-opening parabolas. With $y$ fixed, moving left increases the function. With $x$ fixed, moving away from $y=0$ increases it. Unlabeled contours do not specify which side has larger values; the required test for these choices is perpendicularity, including at off-axis points.\n\nA derivative check gives $\\nabla f=\\left\\langle -1,2y\\right\\rangle$. These directions are normal to the level curves and point toward increasing values. Other curve shapes have a different normal direction somewhere; a reversed field, if offered, points downhill.\n\n![Matching contours and gradient directions](images/08/08b91a07aedb6c0c5a96016aa78dd81527f6f26096783bdd172135aede44373a.png)\n\nTherefore choose this diagram:\n\n![Correct diagram](images/39/398102b2c33d99162a9b99f74b9c8db5b8b4cd84715353c457e58a742c4c17b1.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708229 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18c6e170-bbfc-585c-a454-14a1138bb01f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/3d/3db952065d5ecfade2b25e8a240b05bd876758f0213dfae7f1e436e230eb0d5b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186708221} {:answer/id #uuid "68a692c6-864e-5383-b730-d452113676dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/96/96c710e06e5099240aa2c79b3fb3c857a05e6404e38b204d5cfa20a075c2518c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186708235} {:answer/id #uuid "6c459c39-8b8e-53e9-b6ca-2124ae0cdb17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/78/78e298990e4de0dbc58883097dd88fcbe5338b8169ca34d166c7a395f73eb177.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186708236} {:answer/id #uuid "7dfbc0ee-7881-508b-94db-4c26a3c2f0bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/52/522f72787a12f92cde753ff2bdd86e4bd53e62c995ad29c861b96e3c0ddb22ab.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186708239} {:answer/id #uuid "ffe1bd76-b299-52ff-88b7-113360a08609" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/39/398102b2c33d99162a9b99f74b9c8db5b8b4cd84715353c457e58a742c4c17b1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186708253}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708239} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb250989-29ec-521c-a451-435384d78696" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186708247}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3eda1faa-f1c6-580d-b44f-ef25ca1877d9" :question/problem "The diagram shows the gradient vector field of a function $f(x,y)$. Which of the following could be a contour plot of $f$? Arrows show directions only.\n\n![Given gradient directions](images/ef/ef0f85d081d7330f401f02afbf75847cdcbc9162a6349c819b2e629423581b18.png)" :question/worked-solution "The field must be perpendicular to every regular level curve. One compatible function, used here to check the geometric match, is $f(x,y)=x$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y)&=c\\\\x&=c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThese level curves are parallel straight lines. The labels increase in the normal direction proportional to $(1,0)$. A perpendicular field pointing the opposite way would decrease the function. Unlabeled contours do not specify which side has larger values; the required test for these choices is perpendicularity, including at off-axis points.\n\nA derivative check gives $\\nabla f=\\left\\langle 1,0\\right\\rangle$. These directions are normal to the level curves and point toward increasing values. Other curve shapes have a different normal direction somewhere; a reversed field, if offered, points downhill.\n\n![Matching contours and gradient directions](images/c1/c166d6c0617b4d39620eb32d41403ce2019c035f1adf62070b3ee4dc3be2e0e0.png)\n\nTherefore choose this diagram:\n\n![Correct diagram](images/52/522f72787a12f92cde753ff2bdd86e4bd53e62c995ad29c861b96e3c0ddb22ab.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708238 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11b3e0be-ba6c-55c8-a4e1-b57800902f97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/78/78e298990e4de0dbc58883097dd88fcbe5338b8169ca34d166c7a395f73eb177.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186708220} {:answer/id #uuid "66c26aeb-d810-5f89-95d8-c5834b20ed92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/39/398102b2c33d99162a9b99f74b9c8db5b8b4cd84715353c457e58a742c4c17b1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186708233} {:answer/id #uuid "97e6edeb-db75-513d-bf54-2abd6f772e4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/96/96c710e06e5099240aa2c79b3fb3c857a05e6404e38b204d5cfa20a075c2518c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186708243} {:answer/id #uuid "9c2a3e0e-5979-504e-9b57-5ec077b9e4ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/3d/3db952065d5ecfade2b25e8a240b05bd876758f0213dfae7f1e436e230eb0d5b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186708244} {:answer/id #uuid "fecb3dcb-e573-54b1-b2dc-1fbcd4ebb009" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/52/522f72787a12f92cde753ff2bdd86e4bd53e62c995ad29c861b96e3c0ddb22ab.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186708252}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708220} :answer-field/id #uuid "39a8f7f9-04ca-5357-aafb-3c02ad61c4dd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186708226}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7a70912d-41cc-5550-a275-bf302bc435f2" :question/problem "The diagram shows the gradient vector field of a function $f(x,y)$. Which of the following could be a contour plot of $f$? Arrows show directions only.\n\n![Given gradient directions](images/9c/9cb5ccbef95814c1532b53f0b7a55721ad4a6520dbfe0925d0ce7def8493270c.png)" :question/worked-solution "The field must be perpendicular to every regular level curve. One compatible function, used here to check the geometric match, is $f(x,y)=y^2+\\left(x+1\\right)$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y)&=c\\\\y^2+\\left(x+1\\right)&=c\\\\x&=-1+c-y^2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThese level curves are left-opening parabolas. With $y$ fixed, moving right increases the function. With $x$ fixed, moving away from $y=0$ increases it. Unlabeled contours do not specify which side has larger values; the required test for these choices is perpendicularity, including at off-axis points.\n\nA derivative check gives $\\nabla f=\\left\\langle 1,2y\\right\\rangle$. These directions are normal to the level curves and point toward increasing values. Other curve shapes have a different normal direction somewhere; a reversed field, if offered, points downhill.\n\n![Matching contours and gradient directions](images/03/030525a797176c6e01052f0f3fdefb27bb764f7a821984ab953f0ab96c58aa02.png)\n\nTherefore choose this diagram:\n\n![Correct diagram](images/78/78e298990e4de0dbc58883097dd88fcbe5338b8169ca34d166c7a395f73eb177.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708241 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b214342-0fbc-5c5c-a4cf-dcf07971c016" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/78/78e298990e4de0dbc58883097dd88fcbe5338b8169ca34d166c7a395f73eb177.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186708228} {:answer/id #uuid "47ea83d8-3b9d-5977-abf6-8d9ec33e8592" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/3d/3db952065d5ecfade2b25e8a240b05bd876758f0213dfae7f1e436e230eb0d5b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186708230} {:answer/id #uuid "89cb30ba-10c1-58dc-b3b6-2ecf31a767b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/39/398102b2c33d99162a9b99f74b9c8db5b8b4cd84715353c457e58a742c4c17b1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186708240} {:answer/id #uuid "96a8a788-916e-5026-a66d-7bb107fd26dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/96/96c710e06e5099240aa2c79b3fb3c857a05e6404e38b204d5cfa20a075c2518c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186708242} {:answer/id #uuid "a26bae99-de6a-5aff-8af5-bb1807d18529" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/52/522f72787a12f92cde753ff2bdd86e4bd53e62c995ad29c861b96e3c0ddb22ab.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186708245}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708242} :answer-field/id #uuid "229d87e0-db17-53ab-94ed-84f039a165fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186708223}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9521babf-d362-57ed-8e28-8a7a344eaf46" :question/problem "The diagram shows the gradient vector field of a function $f(x,y)$. Which of the following could be a contour plot of $f$? Arrows show directions only.\n\n![Given gradient directions](images/49/49c2c6963704418fd13bd01a5e822f141e025efecf2fd89d17f9efc9e2065de2.png)" :question/worked-solution "The field must be perpendicular to every regular level curve. One compatible function, used here to check the geometric match, is $f(x,y)=\\left(x+1\\right)y$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y)&=c\\\\\\left(x+1\\right)y&=c\\\\y&=\\frac{c}{\\left(x+1\\right)}\\quad(c\\ne0)\\\\c=0&:\\quad \\left(x+1\\right)=0\\ \\text{or}\\ y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThese level curves are rectangular hyperbolas, with the two intersecting straight lines at the zero level. Relative to $(-1,0)$, increasing the product of the two coordinates increases the function. Unlabeled contours do not specify which side has larger values; the required test for these choices is perpendicularity, including at off-axis points.\n\nA derivative check gives $\\nabla f=\\left\\langle y,\\left(x+1\\right)\\right\\rangle$. These directions are normal to the level curves and point toward increasing values. Other curve shapes have a different normal direction somewhere; a reversed field, if offered, points downhill.\n\n![Matching contours and gradient directions](images/1d/1d5b1da48e12b87e3b37ed324fce6ef1b537194006d95e99c0631580996dc182.png)\n\nTherefore choose this diagram:\n\n![Correct diagram](images/96/96c710e06e5099240aa2c79b3fb3c857a05e6404e38b204d5cfa20a075c2518c.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708256 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f0024e2-ee24-5c9a-bd8f-edaa38094f5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/40/408b83ca3c6ab9b954e78182b59084a390fbfe191f3906a24aee35e2433dd4ec.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186708255} {:answer/id #uuid "14e1b938-67e2-517b-9cc5-4c0a488eb920" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/59/593c5eeb8250533994093b5fdae57cfcad84c3e5fbc8b236c1b9f39b844cfbfe.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186708258} {:answer/id #uuid "558ed151-27df-5ecb-8ff8-df297fc00f29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/04/045ca85388276dfe022d4d028856472f24bc004ceda394bee29222cc1f386d1d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186708264} {:answer/id #uuid "6200cf01-b349-58ae-9b2f-bc84171d072c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/2d/2d6c3502d52395ce5fd28e2421023e21e2af5c75233790f8e90b97b43fe7d55a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186708265} {:answer/id #uuid "f9beee0c-5a73-54e0-aa5d-dfa141dd6c6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/2a/2a9f0100250657d82368ca9a7fa2640a7712a875b911303497ac5997495f3645.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186708275}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708264} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c12920f-ffd5-5000-81b7-0eb59be25247" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186708260}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "12aa0396-e69f-5e3d-ba2f-2e606054f3cc" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)=\\frac{x^2}{2}+y^2$ at the points $(-2,2)$, $(2,1)$, $(1,-2)$?\n\nEach orange dot marks the tail of an arrow. All diagrams use the same scale, with one grid square representing one unit in each direction." :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field is $\\nabla f=f_x\\mathbf{i}+f_y\\mathbf{j}$. First, take the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\nf_x(x,y)=x,\\qquad f_y(x,y)=2y.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y)=x\\mathbf{i}+2y\\mathbf{j}.\n$$\n\nSubstitute the coordinates of each starting point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla f(-2,2)&=(-2)\\mathbf{i}+2(2)\\mathbf{j}=-2\\mathbf{i}+4\\mathbf{j} \\\\ \\nabla f(2,1)&=2\\mathbf{i}+2(1)\\mathbf{j}=2\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j} \\\\ \\nabla f(1,-2)&=\\mathbf{i}+2(-2)\\mathbf{j}=\\mathbf{i}-4\\mathbf{j}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $(-2,2)$ the arrow moves two units left and four units up. At $(2,1)$ it moves two units right and two units up; at $(1,-2)$ it moves one unit right and four units down. Several alternatives have the correct signs, so compare the component lengths as well as direction.\n\nPlace each vector with its tail at the corresponding point. The matching diagram is:\n\n![](images/04/045ca85388276dfe022d4d028856472f24bc004ceda394bee29222cc1f386d1d.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708263 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1cc3179c-5c09-57b0-ab6b-b52661770295" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/02/022784c8aa196e90cf402ec16140b93810f6df24d374d21464391e5f32341dd2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186708259} {:answer/id #uuid "984813ce-3d72-5951-b3b1-a25491d5f558" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/58/58fbeb90aeabe69f23aa45071b29a4c65ba9a83570982f4936ee96222ef1f905.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186708268} {:answer/id #uuid "a3d6f9ef-39d4-5fd2-931c-f04bcbdd57d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/96/96701b4e35a841cef0215dc8f7e99bf54c15dac11d966b6c7674addf01dcd0d7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186708269} {:answer/id #uuid "c89336b7-fa14-502f-8ccd-95060ed976cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/f0/f0481e1fc70cc7d3ed91e70b549ea57e3cfd3208528c7152b45c4f55b160b670.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186708270} {:answer/id #uuid "e4059277-9bc2-596b-b30a-b9e9ea381ad1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/fc/fc607f6ab6ff769d832cbddc82fab73d845f8fc769fb319b55458cf09d2ad7db.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186708274}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708274} :answer-field/id #uuid "d39e6543-e613-5bdf-b902-cffa96b0130f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186708271}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "53281498-398f-5f06-84aa-842b0d80aaac" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)=\\frac{(x-y)^2}{2}$ at the points $(2,1)$, $(1,2)$, $(1,-1)$?\n\nEach orange dot marks the tail of an arrow. All diagrams use the same scale, with one grid square representing one unit in each direction." :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field is $\\nabla f=f_x\\mathbf{i}+f_y\\mathbf{j}$. First, take the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(x,y)&=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot2(x-y)\\cdot1=x-y \\\\ f_y(x,y)&=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot2(x-y)\\cdot(-1)=-(x-y)\\end{aligned}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y)=(x-y)\\mathbf{i}-(x-y)\\mathbf{j}.\n$$\n\nSubstitute the coordinates of each starting point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla f(2,1)&=(2-1)\\mathbf{i}-(2-1)\\mathbf{j}=\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j} \\\\ \\nabla f(1,2)&=(1-2)\\mathbf{i}-(1-2)\\mathbf{j}=-\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j} \\\\ \\nabla f(1,-1)&=(1-(-1))\\mathbf{i}-(1-(-1))\\mathbf{j}=2\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first two arrows have equal lengths and opposite directions. The arrow at $(1,-1)$ points down and right and is twice as long as the arrow at $(2,1)$. Every alternative agrees with some of these arrows, so check the vectors at all three starting points.\n\nPlace each vector with its tail at the corresponding point. The matching diagram is:\n\n![](images/fc/fc607f6ab6ff769d832cbddc82fab73d845f8fc769fb319b55458cf09d2ad7db.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708273 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13b76eec-d3ee-5b74-9501-310f6979024f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/e9/e9b9bc65e813da9cb89f9bbabedc10a83b8da46ad13dfef03568ec204ee9e701.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186708257} {:answer/id #uuid "48d61110-0b29-5075-83fa-44219d741b80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/f8/f8a0e4689da26598fbca83d7b0c98548af04476d54c2173f4548959fbca7a5ed.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186708262} {:answer/id #uuid "6e46e739-a8cc-5605-b7bd-a47314befc26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/9c/9cc388531924637f3e6aefa88369a0bf53710f36afac15b8fd608af581de398f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186708266} {:answer/id #uuid "77bbe8fe-f5f2-54e5-8c6e-601b50d841c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/7a/7a723a8cb0666cfb08f26bc29f2354e03aa97e5f29bd6f4c4a1db1acd64b79ac.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186708267} {:answer/id #uuid "d3c1f166-bffe-59d0-bc28-60dc12129ee5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/21/21eacab25ca4f453e33cabc5f39c9eb9bd528eb4478e7fba6fe973d8191d4ef5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186708272}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708257} :answer-field/id #uuid "381c13ac-a993-5437-808d-3ad5cacf8bf6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186708261}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "db160ca5-75d5-5303-b8c6-794b292a351d" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)=x+2y$ at the points $(-2,2)$, $(2,2)$, $(2,-2)$?\n\nEach orange dot marks the tail of an arrow. All diagrams use the same scale, with one grid square representing one unit in each direction." :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field is $\\nabla f=f_x\\mathbf{i}+f_y\\mathbf{j}$. First, take the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\nf_x(x,y)=1,\\qquad f_y(x,y)=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y)=\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}.\n$$\n\nSubstitute the coordinates of each starting point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla f(-2,2)&=\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j} \\\\ \\nabla f(2,2)&=\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j} \\\\ \\nabla f(2,-2)&=\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe gradient is constant: every arrow moves one unit to the right and two units up, regardless of its starting point. An arrow moving two units right and one unit up interchanges the components.\n\nPlace each vector with its tail at the corresponding point. The matching diagram is:\n\n![](images/e9/e9b9bc65e813da9cb89f9bbabedc10a83b8da46ad13dfef03568ec204ee9e701.png)"}]}
